inference_trace:
Approach: strip the surrounding parentheses to get the digit string. Try every split point into a left part and a right part. For each part, list all valid ways to write it as a number, then output every combination as `"(a, b)"`.

For a digit string `t`, try each decimal position `d` from 1 to `len` (where `d = len` means no decimal point):
- The integer part `t[0, d)` is valid only if it is `"0"` or does not start with `'0'`.
- The fractional part `t[d, len)`, when present, must not end with `'0'`.

Output strings use the format `"(a, b)"` with no trailing space. The spaces shown in the examples are formatting artifacts of the problem statement.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<String> ambiguousCoordinates(String s) {
        String t = s.trim();
        t = t.substring(1, t.length() - 1);
        List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 1; i < t.length(); i++) {
            List<String> a = gen(t.substring(0, i));
            List<String> b = gen(t.substring(i));
            for (String x : a) {
                for (String y : b) {
                    res.add("(" + x + ", " + y + ")");
                }
            }
        }
        return res;
    }

    private List<String> gen(String t) {
        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = t.length();
        for (int d = 1; d <= n; d++) {
            String ip = t.substring(0, d);
            String fp = t.substring(d);
            if (ip.length() > 1 && ip.charAt(0) == '0') continue;
            if (fp.length() > 0 && fp.charAt(fp.length() - 1) == '0') continue;
            out.add(fp.isEmpty() ? ip : ip + "." + fp);
        }
        return out;
    }
}
```

