inference_trace:
Sort the array so every possible child value comes before its parent. Let `dp[i]` be the number of trees whose root is `arr[i]`.

- Each value alone is one tree, so `dp[i]` starts at 1.
- For every smaller value `arr[j]` that divides `arr[i]`, check whether the quotient `arr[i] / arr[j]` is also in the array, using a map from value to index.
- If it is at index `k`, add `dp[j] * dp[k]` to `dp[i]`. This counts trees with left child `arr[j]` and right child `arr[k]`.
- Iterating over every `j` covers both orderings of the two children.
- Use `long` arithmetic and take results modulo 10^9 + 7.

The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]`. The double loop runs in O(n²), which is fine for n ≤ 1000.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int numFactoredBinaryTrees(int[] arr) {
        final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        int n = arr.length;
        Map<Integer, Integer> idx = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) idx.put(arr[i], i);
        long[] dp = new long[n];
        long total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = 1;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (arr[i] % arr[j] == 0) {
                    Integer k = idx.get(arr[i] / arr[j]);
                    if (k != null) {
                        dp[i] = (dp[i] + dp[j] * dp[k]) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }
            total = (total + dp[i]) % MOD;
        }
        return (int) total;
    }
}
```

