inference_trace:
Track the index of the most recent `1`. Each time another `1` appears, the number of zeros between the two is `i - prev - 1`. If that count is less than `k`, return `false`. If the loop finishes without a violation, return `true`.

Example 2 shows the check: the `1`s at indices 3 and 5 have only one zero between them, which is less than `k = 2`.

This is a single pass, so it runs in O(n) time with O(1) extra space.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean kLengthApart(int[] nums, int k) {
        int prev = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == 1) {
                if (prev != -1 && i - prev - 1 < k) return false;
                prev = i;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

