inference_trace:
Leading 'L' cars drive off to the left and never collide. Trailing 'R' cars drive off to the right and never collide.

Every other moving car (any 'L' or 'R' between those boundaries) eventually stops. Each such car contributes exactly 1 collision count:
- In an R-L head-on collision, the pair adds 2, which is 1 per moving car.
- When a moving car hits a stationary car, it adds 1 for that moving car.

So the answer is the number of non-'S' characters after stripping leading 'L's and trailing 'R's.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countCollisions(String directions) {
        int n = directions.length();
        int i = 0, j = n - 1;
        while (i < n && directions.charAt(i) == 'L') i++;
        while (j >= 0 && directions.charAt(j) == 'R') j--;
        int count = 0;
        for (int k = i; k <= j; k++) {
            if (directions.charAt(k) != 'S') count++;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

