inference_trace:
With at most 16 elements, a simple DFS over all subsets is fast enough.

1. The maximum OR is the OR of all elements, since adding an element can never lower an OR. Call it `max`.
2. Run a DFS over the indices. At each index, either include the element or skip it, carrying the running OR value.
3. When the DFS reaches the end of the array, return 1 if the running OR equals `max`, otherwise 0.
4. Sum these counts over both branches.

The empty subset has OR 0. Since every `nums[i] >= 1`, `max` is at least 1, so the empty subset is never counted.

```java
class Solution {
    int max;
    int[] a;
    public int countMaxOrSubsets(int[] nums) {
        a = nums;
        max = 0;
        for (int x : nums) max |= x;
        return dfs(0, 0);
    }
    private int dfs(int i, int cur) {
        if (i == a.length) return cur == max ? 1 : 0;
        return dfs(i + 1, cur | a[i]) + dfs(i + 1, cur);
    }
}
```

