inference_trace:
With `word.length <= 100`, a simple O(n²) brute force is fast enough.

1. Fix each start index `i`.
2. Extend the end index `j` to the right one character at a time.
3. If `word[j]` is not a vowel, stop extending from this `i`, since any longer substring would also contain a consonant.
4. Otherwise, record the vowel in a 5-bit mask (one bit per vowel).
5. Whenever the mask equals `31` (all five bits set), the substring `word[i..j]` contains all five vowels, so increment the count.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countVowelSubstrings(String word) {
        int n = word.length(), count = 0;
        String v = "aeiou";
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int mask = 0;
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int idx = v.indexOf(word.charAt(j));
                if (idx < 0) break;
                mask |= 1 << idx;
                if (mask == 31) count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

