Approach: do a DFS that records the depth and parent of the nodes holding `x` and `y`. They are cousins exactly when the depths match and the parents differ.

1. Start the DFS at the root with depth 0 and parent `null`.
2. When a node's value equals `x`, store its depth and parent; do the same for `y`.
3. Recurse into both children with depth + 1 and the current node as parent.
4. After the traversal, return `true` if `xDepth == yDepth` and `xParent != yParent`.

The tree is visited once, so this runs in O(n) time and O(h) stack space, where h is the tree height.

```java
class Solution {
    private int xDepth = -1, yDepth = -2;
    private TreeNode xParent = null, yParent = null;

    public boolean isCousins(TreeNode root, int x, int y) {
        dfs(root, null, 0, x, y);
        return xDepth == yDepth && xParent != yParent;
    }

    private void dfs(TreeNode node, TreeNode parent, int depth, int x, int y) {
        if (node == null) return;
        if (node.val == x) {
            xDepth = depth;
            xParent = parent;
        } else if (node.val == y) {
            yDepth = depth;
            yParent = parent;
        }
        dfs(node.left, node, depth + 1, x, y);
        dfs(node.right, node, depth + 1, x, y);
    }
}
```

