Use a lazy increment array so every operation runs in O(1).

- Keep the stack values in an array `stack`, with `top` as the current size.
- Keep a second array `inc`, where `inc[i]` is a pending increment for element `i` and every element below it.
- **push(x):** if `top < maxSize`, store `x` at `stack[top]` and increase `top`.
- **pop():** if the stack is empty, return `-1`. Otherwise decrease `top` and return `stack[top] + inc[top]`. If `top > 0`, add `inc[top]` into `inc[top - 1]` so the pending increment passes to the elements below. Then clear `inc[top]`.
- **increment(k, val):** let `i = min(k, top) - 1`. If `i >= 0`, add `val` to `inc[i]`. This marks the bottom `min(k, top)` elements without touching each one.

```java
class CustomStack {
    private int[] stack;
    private int[] inc;
    private int top;
    private int maxSize;

    public CustomStack(int maxSize) {
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
        stack = new int[maxSize];
        inc = new int[maxSize];
        top = 0;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        if (top < maxSize) {
            stack[top] = x;
            inc[top] = 0;
            top++;
        }
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == 0) return -1;
        top--;
        int res = stack[top] + inc[top];
        if (top > 0) inc[top - 1] += inc[top];
        inc[top] = 0;
        return res;
    }

    public void increment(int k, int val) {
        int i = Math.min(k, top) - 1;
        if (i >= 0) inc[i] += val;
    }
}
```

