The two operations limit what can change, so two strings are close exactly when three conditions hold:

1. **Same length.** Neither operation changes the length of a string.
2. **Same set of distinct characters.** Operation 2 only swaps between characters that already exist, so no new character can appear and none can disappear.
3. **Same multiset of frequencies.** Operation 1 can reorder characters freely. Operation 2 swaps the counts of two characters, which permutes the frequency list but keeps its values.

So the approach is:
- Count the occurrences of each letter in both words.
- For every letter, check that it is either present in both words or absent from both.
- Sort both count arrays and check they are equal.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean closeStrings(String word1, String word2) {
        if (word1.length() != word2.length()) return false;
        int[] a = new int[26], b = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < word1.length(); i++) a[word1.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        for (int i = 0; i < word2.length(); i++) b[word2.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if ((a[i] == 0) != (b[i] == 0)) return false;
        }
        java.util.Arrays.sort(a);
        java.util.Arrays.sort(b);
        return java.util.Arrays.equals(a, b);
    }
}
```

