Use a greedy two-pointer approach with `lo = 0` and `hi = n`.

- For each character, if it is `'I'`, assign `lo` and increment it. Every number still unused is larger than `lo`, so the next element is guaranteed to be bigger.
- If it is `'D'`, assign `hi` and decrement it. Every number still unused is smaller than `hi`, so the next element is guaranteed to be smaller.
- After processing all `n` characters, `lo == hi`, and that single remaining value fills the last position.

This runs in O(n) time.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] diStringMatch(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        int[] res = new int[n + 1];
        int lo = 0, hi = n;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == 'I') {
                res[i] = lo++;
            } else {
                res[i] = hi--;
            }
        }
        res[n] = lo;
        return res;
    }
}
```

