Make one left-to-right pass and keep track of the next index not yet added to the result, called `start`.

1. When `nums[j] == key`, every index from `j - k` to `j + k` (clipped to the array bounds) is a k-distant index.
2. Begin adding at `max(start, j - k)` so indices already added are not repeated.
3. Add each index up to `min(n - 1, j + k)`, then set `start` to the index after the last one added.

Because `j` increases and each range starts at or after `start`, the indices are added in increasing order with no duplicates. Each index is added at most once, so the total work is O(n).

The starter code uses `List` without an import. LeetCode imports `java.util` automatically, but I add `import java.util.*;` at the top for safety. It sits outside the class, so the required class and method shape is unchanged.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> findKDistantIndices(int[] nums, int key, int k) {
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = nums.length;
        int start = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[j] == key) {
                int lo = Math.max(start, j - k);
                int hi = Math.min(n - 1, j + k);
                for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++) res.add(i);
                start = Math.max(start, hi + 1);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

