Approach: reverse the graph and peel off safe nodes with Kahn's algorithm. This avoids recursion, so deep chains cannot overflow the stack.

1. Let `outdeg[i]` be the number of outgoing edges of node `i`. Terminal nodes have `outdeg = 0`, so they are safe and go into a queue first.
2. Build the reverse graph: for each edge `i → j`, record `i` as a predecessor of `j`.
3. Pop a safe node `v` from the queue. For each predecessor `p` of `v`, decrement `outdeg[p]`. When it reaches 0, every edge out of `p` leads to a safe node, so `p` is safe and is added to the queue.
4. Nodes never marked safe can reach a cycle and are unsafe.
5. Collect safe nodes by scanning indices from 0 to n−1, which gives ascending order.

The reverse graph is stored in CSR form (`start` offsets plus a flat `rev` array) to keep it compact. The total cost is O(n + edges).

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.length;
        int[] outdeg = new int[n];
        int[] cnt = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            outdeg[i] = graph[i].length;
            for (int j : graph[i]) cnt[j + 1]++;
        }
        int[] start = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) start[i + 1] = start[i] + cnt[i + 1];
        int[] pos = Arrays.copyOf(start, n + 1);
        int[] rev = new int[start[n]];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j : graph[i]) rev[pos[j]++] = i;
        }
        int[] queue = new int[n];
        int head = 0, tail = 0;
        boolean[] safe = new boolean[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (outdeg[i] == 0) {
                queue[tail++] = i;
                safe[i] = true;
            }
        }
        while (head < tail) {
            int v = queue[head++];
            for (int k = start[v]; k < start[v + 1]; k++) {
                int p = rev[k];
                if (--outdeg[p] == 0) {
                    safe[p] = true;
                    queue[tail++] = p;
                }
            }
        }
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (safe[i]) res.add(i);
        return res;
    }
}
```

