Use a DP table where `dp[i][k]` means the frog can stand on stone `i` having arrived with a jump of `k` units.

**Bound on k.** Reaching stone `i` takes at most `i` jumps, and each jump grows by at most 1. So any jump landing on stone `i` is at most `i`, and `k` never needs to exceed `n`.

**Transition.** For each pair `j < i`, let `k = stones[i] - stones[j]`.
- If `k > j + 1`, stop scanning `j` for this `i`. The last jump into stone `j` is at most `j`, so the next jump is at most `j + 1`.
- Iterate `j` from `i - 1` downward. As `j` decreases, `k` increases and `j` decreases, so once `k > j + 1` it stays true and we can break.
- Otherwise, set `dp[i][k]` to true if any of `dp[j][k-1]`, `dp[j][k]`, `dp[j][k+1]` is true.

**Base case.** Set `dp[0][0] = true`. From stone 0 the only jump allowed is `k = 1`, and that is covered by `dp[0][k-1]` with `k - 1 = 0`. A jump of `k = 2` from stone 0 would need `dp[0][1]` or `dp[0][2]`, both false, so it is correctly rejected. The `k > j + 1` break (here `j + 1 = 1`) also rejects it.

**Overflow.** Positions can be up to 2^31 - 1, but a difference of two non-negative ints always fits in an int, so `k` is safe. The break guarantees `k <= j + 1 <= n`, so `k + 1` stays within an array of size `n + 2`.

**Answer.** Return true if any `dp[n-1][k]` is true; this can be returned as soon as such an entry is set. The time complexity is O(n²) and memory is a 2000×2002 boolean table, about 4 MB.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canCross(int[] stones) {
        int n = stones.length;
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n + 2];
        dp[0][0] = true;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                long diff = (long) stones[i] - stones[j];
                if (diff > j + 1) break;
                int k = (int) diff;
                if (dp[j][k - 1] || dp[j][k] || dp[j][k + 1]) {
                    dp[i][k] = true;
                    if (i == n - 1) return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

