Approach: use prefix sums so each row, column and diagonal sum of a candidate square costs O(1), then try sizes from largest to smallest.

1. **Prefix sums.** Build four arrays:
   - `row[i][j+1]`: prefix sum along row `i`.
   - `col[i+1][j]`: prefix sum along column `j`.
   - `d1[i+1][j+1] = d1[i][j] + grid[i][j]`: prefix sum along the main diagonal direction.
   - `d2[i+1][j] = d2[i][j+1] + grid[i][j]`: prefix sum along the anti-diagonal direction.

2. **Diagonal sums for a square** with top-left `(r, c)` and size `k`:
   - Main diagonal covers cells `(r+t, c+t)`, so its sum is `d1[r+k][c+k] - d1[r][c]`.
   - Anti-diagonal covers cells `(r+t, c+k-1-t)`. Since `d2[i+1][j]` accumulates cells `(i, j), (i-1, j+1), ...`:
     - The bottom-left cell is `(r+k-1, c)`, giving `d2[r+k][c]`.
     - The cell just before the top-right one is `(r-1, c+k)`, giving `d2[r][c+k]`.
     - So the anti-diagonal sum is `d2[r+k][c] - d2[r][c+k]`.

3. **Search.** Loop `k` from `min(m, n)` down to 2. For each position, take the main diagonal sum as the target and compare the anti-diagonal, every row and every column against it. Return `k` on the first match; otherwise return 1.

4. **Overflow.** Values go up to 10^6 and a line has at most 50 cells, so sums reach 5e7, which fits in an `int`.

5. **Complexity.** O(min(m, n) · m · n · k), roughly 50^4 ≈ 6.25M operations, which is fine.

```java
class Solution {
    public int largestMagicSquare(int[][] grid) {
        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
        int[][] row = new int[m][n + 1];
        int[][] col = new int[m + 1][n];
        int[][] d1 = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        int[][] d2 = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                row[i][j + 1] = row[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                col[i + 1][j] = col[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                d1[i + 1][j + 1] = d1[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                d2[i + 1][j] = d2[i][j + 1] + grid[i][j];
            }
        }
        for (int k = Math.min(m, n); k >= 2; k--) {
            for (int r = 0; r + k <= m; r++) {
                for (int c = 0; c + k <= n; c++) {
                    int target = d1[r + k][c + k] - d1[r][c];
                    if (d2[r + k][c] - d2[r][c + k] != target) continue;
                    boolean ok = true;
                    for (int t = 0; t < k && ok; t++) {
                        if (row[r + t][c + k] - row[r + t][c] != target) ok = false;
                        else if (col[r + k][c + t] - col[r][c + t] != target) ok = false;
                    }
                    if (ok) return k;
                }
            }
        }
        return 1;
    }
}
```

