Approach: walk the string from the end so that every group except the first gets exactly `k` characters.

1. Iterate `s` from the last character to the first and skip dashes.
2. Before appending a character, if the number of characters already collected is a positive multiple of `k`, append a dash first.
3. Append the character in uppercase.
4. Reverse the builder at the end. Because dashes are only added between characters, there is never a leading or trailing dash.
5. If `s` is all dashes, nothing is appended and the result is the empty string.

The example strings appear to have trailing spaces, but the constraints say `s` contains only letters, digits and dashes. So no space handling is needed.

```java
class Solution {
    public String licenseKeyFormatting(String s, int k) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c == '-') continue;
            if (cnt > 0 && cnt % k == 0) sb.append('-');
            sb.append(Character.toUpperCase(c));
            cnt++;
        }
        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```

