Approach: mark every value in `nums`, then walk the list once and count where each component ends.

1. Values are in `[0, n)`, and `nums` values are also below `n`, so a boolean array of size `10001` can mark membership.
2. Traverse the linked list. A node ends a component when its value is in the set and either the next node is null or the next node's value is not in the set.
3. Each component has exactly one such end node, so the count of end nodes is the answer.

This runs in O(n) time and uses O(n) extra space for the boolean array.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numComponents(ListNode head, int[] nums) {
        boolean[] in = new boolean[10001];
        for (int x : nums) in[x] = true;
        int count = 0;
        ListNode cur = head;
        while (cur != null) {
            if (in[cur.val] && (cur.next == null || !in[cur.next.val])) count++;
            cur = cur.next;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

