inference_trace:
Use patience sorting with binary search, which runs in O(n log n).

Keep an array `tails`, where `tails[i]` is the smallest possible tail value of an increasing subsequence of length `i + 1`.

For each number `x`:
- Binary search for the first index whose value is `>= x` (the lower bound).
- If no such index exists, `x` extends the longest subsequence, so append it.
- Otherwise, replace the value at that index with `x`, giving a smaller tail for that length.

Using the lower bound (`>=` rather than `>`) keeps the subsequence strictly increasing, since an equal value replaces rather than extends.

The final size of `tails` is the length of the longest increasing subsequence.

```java
class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int[] tails = new int[nums.length];
        int size = 0;
        for (int x : nums) {
            int lo = 0, hi = size;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
                if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
                else hi = mid;
            }
            tails[lo] = x;
            if (lo == size) size++;
        }
        return size;
    }
}
```

