Sort the words by length so every predecessor is processed before any longer word that could follow it. Then use a map from each word to the length of the longest chain ending at that word.

For each word, try deleting each of its characters. If the resulting shorter string is already in the map, it is a predecessor, so the chain ending at the current word can be one longer than that predecessor's chain. Store the best value for the word and track the overall maximum.

With up to 1000 words of length at most 16, this does about 16 deletions per word, each costing O(16) to build the substring. That is fast enough.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int longestStrChain(String[] words) {
        Arrays.sort(words, (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length());
        Map<String, Integer> dp = new HashMap<>();
        int best = 0;
        for (String w : words) {
            int cur = 1;
            for (int i = 0; i < w.length(); i++) {
                String p = w.substring(0, i) + w.substring(i + 1);
                Integer v = dp.get(p);
                if (v != null && v + 1 > cur) cur = v + 1;
            }
            Integer old = dp.get(w);
            if (old == null || old < cur) dp.put(w, cur);
            best = Math.max(best, cur);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

