inference_trace:
For each point, group every other point by the slope of the line joining them. The largest group, plus the anchor point itself, is the best line through that anchor. The answer is the maximum over all anchors.

**Slope representation.** To avoid floating-point error, store each slope as a reduced fraction:

- Compute `dx` and `dy`, then divide both by `gcd(|dx|, |dy|)`.
- Normalize the sign so `dx` is positive. If `dx` is 0, set `dy` to 1 (vertical line).
- Since the points are unique, `dx` and `dy` are never both 0.

**Hash key.** Coordinates are at most 10^4, so the reduced `dx` and `dy` are bounded by 2·10^4. That lets us pack the pair into a single long key: `dx * 100000 + dy`.

**Complexity.** O(n² log C) time, which is fine for n ≤ 300.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int maxPoints(int[][] points) {
        int n = points.length;
        if (n <= 2) return n;
        int best = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            HashMap<Long, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
            int local = 0;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int dx = points[j][0] - points[i][0];
                int dy = points[j][1] - points[i][1];
                int g = gcd(Math.abs(dx), Math.abs(dy));
                dx /= g;
                dy /= g;
                if (dx < 0 || (dx == 0 && dy < 0)) {
                    dx = -dx;
                    dy = -dy;
                }
                long key = (long) dx * 100000L + dy;
                int c = map.merge(key, 1, Integer::sum);
                local = Math.max(local, c);
            }
            best = Math.max(best, local + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int t = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = t;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

