Approach:

1. Find the index of the first `'0'`. If there is none, the string is already all `1`s, so return it unchanged.
2. Count the zeros, call it `zeros`. Operation 2 lets every zero after the first one be pulled left, so all zeros can be gathered into one block starting at that first-zero position.
3. Operation 1 turns `"00"` into `"10"`, so a block of `zeros` zeros becomes `zeros - 1` ones followed by a single `0`.
4. The result is all `'1'`s except for one `'0'` at position `first + zeros - 1`.

For example, `"000110"` has its first zero at index 0 and 4 zeros, so the single `0` lands at index 3, giving `"111011"`.

To be safe, any non-`0`/`1` characters such as spaces are ignored. The constraints say only `0` and `1` appear, so this is just defensive.

```java
class Solution {
    public String maximumBinaryString(String binary) {
        int n = binary.length();
        int first = binary.indexOf('0');
        if (first < 0) return binary;
        int zeros = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (binary.charAt(i) == '0') zeros++;
        char[] res = new char[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) res[i] = '1';
        res[first + zeros - 1] = '0';
        return new String(res);
    }
}
```

