Use a bitmask DP over the set of mentors already assigned.

1. Precompute `score[i][j]`, the compatibility of student `i` with mentor `j`.
2. Let `dp[mask]` be the best total when the mentors in `mask` have been assigned to the first `popcount(mask)` students.
3. For each reachable `mask`, the next student is `i = bitCount(mask)`. Try every mentor `j` not in `mask` and update `dp[mask | 1<<j]` with `dp[mask] + score[i][j]`.
4. The answer is `dp[(1<<m) - 1]`, when all mentors are used.

With `m <= 8`, there are at most 256 masks and 8 choices per mask, so this is very fast.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxCompatibilitySum(int[][] students, int[][] mentors) {
        int m = students.length, n = students[0].length;
        int[][] score = new int[m][m];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++)
                for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)
                    if (students[i][k] == mentors[j][k]) score[i][j]++;
        int full = 1 << m;
        int[] dp = new int[full];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, -1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
            int i = Integer.bitCount(mask);
            if (i >= m) continue;
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << j)) != 0) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << j);
                dp[nm] = Math.max(dp[nm], dp[mask] + score[i][j]);
            }
        }
        return dp[full - 1];
    }
}
```

