inference_trace:
Approach: scan the prefixes once, keeping two maps:
- `cnt[x]`: how many times value `x` has appeared so far.
- `freq[f]`: how many distinct values currently appear exactly `f` times.

Let `maxF` be the largest count seen so far, and let `len = i + 1` be the length of the current prefix. The prefix is valid if any of these holds:

1. **`maxF == 1`**: every value appears once. Removing any element leaves all counts equal to 1, or leaves an empty array.
2. **`freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len`**: all values share count `maxF` except one value that appears once. Removing that single occurrence leaves all counts equal to `maxF`.
3. **`freq[maxF - 1] * (maxF - 1) + maxF == len`**: exactly one value has count `maxF` and all others have `maxF - 1`. Removing one occurrence of the `maxF` value makes all counts `maxF - 1`.
   - If there is only one distinct value, `freq[maxF - 1]` is 0 and the condition reduces to `maxF == len`. This is still valid, since removing one element leaves a single value with equal count.
   - When `maxF == 1`, case 1 already covers it.

Whenever a condition holds, record `len` as the best answer so far.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxEqualFreq(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] cnt = new int[100001];
        int[] freq = new int[n + 2];
        int maxF = 0, ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int x = nums[i];
            if (cnt[x] > 0) freq[cnt[x]]--;
            cnt[x]++;
            freq[cnt[x]]++;
            maxF = Math.max(maxF, cnt[x]);
            int len = i + 1;
            if (maxF == 1
                || (long) freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len
                || (long) freq[maxF - 1] * (maxF - 1) + maxF == len) {
                ans = len;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

