The examples show trailing spaces, which are likely just formatting artifacts. To be safe, the solution scans character by character and only counts a word when it is non-empty, so extra spaces are harmless.

1. Mark each broken letter (only `a`–`z`) in a boolean array, ignoring any other characters.
2. Walk through `text`, tracking whether the current word has any letters and whether it contains a broken letter.
3. At each space, or at the end of the string, count the word if it is non-empty and has no broken letters. Then reset both flags.

```java
class Solution {
    public int canBeTypedWords(String text, String brokenLetters) {
        boolean[] broken = new boolean[26];
        for (char c : brokenLetters.toCharArray()) {
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') broken[c - 'a'] = true;
        }
        int count = 0;
        boolean hasLetters = false, bad = false;
        for (int i = 0; i <= text.length(); i++) {
            char c = i < text.length() ? text.charAt(i) : ' ';
            if (c == ' ') {
                if (hasLetters && !bad) count++;
                hasLetters = false;
                bad = false;
            } else {
                hasLetters = true;
                if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' && broken[c - 'a']) bad = true;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

