Approach: put every point in a hash set, then treat each pair of points as possible opposite corners of a rectangle.

1. Encode each point as a single `long` key `x * 40001 + y` and store all keys in a `HashSet`.
2. For every pair of points `p1 = (x1, y1)` and `p2 = (x2, y2)` with `x1 != x2` and `y1 != y2`, they can be diagonal corners of an axis-parallel rectangle.
3. The rectangle exists if the other two corners, `(x1, y2)` and `(x2, y1)`, are both in the set.
4. When it exists, its area is `|x1 - x2| * |y1 - y2|`; keep the minimum.
5. If no rectangle is found, return `0`.

With at most 500 points there are about 125k pairs, each checked in O(1), so the total time is O(n²).

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int minAreaRect(int[][] points) {
        Set<Long> set = new HashSet<>();
        for (int[] p : points) set.add((long) p[0] * 40001 + p[1]);
        int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int n = points.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int x1 = points[i][0], y1 = points[i][1];
                int x2 = points[j][0], y2 = points[j][1];
                if (x1 == x2 || y1 == y2) continue;
                int area = Math.abs(x1 - x2) * Math.abs(y1 - y2);
                if (area >= best) continue;
                if (set.contains((long) x1 * 40001 + y2) && set.contains((long) x2 * 40001 + y1)) {
                    best = area;
                }
            }
        }
        return best == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? 0 : best;
    }
}
```

