BFS is safer than recursion here, since the tree can have up to 10^5 nodes and could be a skewed chain that overflows the stack.

Approach:
1. If the root is null, the depth is 0.
2. Traverse the tree level by level, starting at depth 1.
3. The first leaf found (a node with no children) is the nearest leaf, so return the current depth.
4. Otherwise, add the node's non-null children to the queue and move to the next level.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public int minDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        java.util.ArrayDeque<TreeNode> q = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        q.add(root);
        int depth = 1;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode n = q.poll();
                if (n.left == null && n.right == null) return depth;
                if (n.left != null) q.add(n.left);
                if (n.right != null) q.add(n.right);
            }
            depth++;
        }
        return depth;
    }
}
```

