inference_trace:
Use memoized recursion on n. The key observation is that eating one orange at a time is only useful for reaching the nearest multiple of 2 or 3.

- To halve n, first eat `n % 2` single oranges, then take the halving step. That costs `n % 2 + 1` days and leaves `n / 2`.
- To reduce n to a third, first eat `n % 3` single oranges, then take the thirds step. That costs `n % 3 + 1` days and leaves `n / 3`.

So the recurrence is:

`f(n) = min(n % 2 + 1 + f(n / 2), n % 3 + 1 + f(n / 3))`

with base cases `f(0) = 0` and `f(1) = 1`.

Every reachable state has the form `n / (2^a · 3^b)`. That gives only about O(log² n) distinct states, so a HashMap memo is enough.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    private Map<Integer, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public int minDays(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        Integer c = memo.get(n);
        if (c != null) return c;
        int res = Math.min(n % 2 + 1 + minDays(n / 2), n % 3 + 1 + minDays(n / 3));
        memo.put(n, res);
        return res;
    }
}
```

