inference_trace:
Scan the string left to right while tracking the number of currently unmatched `'['` brackets in `open`:

- On `'['`, increment `open`.
- On `']'`, if `open > 0`, it closes a bracket, so decrement `open`.
- Otherwise, count it as an unmatched `']'` by incrementing `unmatched`.

All the unmatched `']'` brackets end up as a block of `k` closers at the front of the reduced string. One swap moves a `']'` from the front with a `'['` from the back, and this fixes two unmatched pairs at once. So the answer is `(k + 1) / 2`.

Checking the examples:
- `"]]][[["` gives `k = 3`, so `(3 + 1) / 2 = 2`.
- `"][]["` gives `k = 1`, so `(1 + 1) / 2 = 1`.

The scan is O(n) time with O(1) extra space.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minSwaps(String s) {
        int open = 0, unmatched = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c == '[') {
                open++;
            } else {
                if (open > 0) open--;
                else unmatched++;
            }
        }
        return (unmatched + 1) / 2;
    }
}
```

