inference_trace:
Use a bitmask DP over subsets of completed tasks. For each mask, store a pair `(sessions, lastLoad)`:

- `sessions` is the number of sessions used, with the current session counted as open.
- `lastLoad` is the time already used in the current session.

The pairs are compared lexicographically: fewer sessions is better, and for equal sessions a smaller current load is better.

Transitions: for each task `i` not yet in the mask:

- If `load + tasks[i] <= sessionTime`, the task joins the current session, giving `(s, load + t)`.
- Otherwise it starts a new session, giving `(s + 1, t)`.

Base case: `dp[0] = (1, 0)`, meaning one session is open with no load. The answer is the session count of `dp[full]`.

This greedy-ordered DP is the known correct approach for this problem. The state space is 2^14 masks with 14 transitions each, which is fast.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minSessions(int[] tasks, int sessionTime) {
        int n = tasks.length;
        int full = 1 << n;
        int[] ses = new int[full];
        int[] load = new int[full];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(ses, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        ses[0] = 1;
        load[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (ses[mask] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                int s, l;
                if (load[mask] + tasks[i] <= sessionTime) {
                    s = ses[mask];
                    l = load[mask] + tasks[i];
                } else {
                    s = ses[mask] + 1;
                    l = tasks[i];
                }
                if (s < ses[nm] || (s == ses[nm] && l < load[nm])) {
                    ses[nm] = s;
                    load[nm] = l;
                }
            }
        }
        return ses[full - 1];
    }
}
```

