Use a monotonic stack and loop over the array twice so the search can wrap around.

1. Fill the result array with `-1`, which covers elements with no greater number.
2. Iterate `i` from `0` to `2n - 1` and work with index `i % n`.
3. While the stack is not empty and `nums[stack.top] < nums[i % n]`, pop the top index and set its result to `nums[i % n]`. The stack holds indices whose next greater number is still unknown.
4. Push indices only during the first pass (`i < n`). The second pass exists only to resolve the wrap-around cases.

Each index is pushed and popped at most once, so the time is O(n) and the extra space is O(n).

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] res = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(res, -1);
        int[] stack = new int[n];
        int top = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            int v = nums[i % n];
            while (top >= 0 && nums[stack[top]] < v) {
                res[stack[top--]] = v;
            }
            if (i < n) stack[++top] = i;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

