The approach is to convert each date into a day count from a fixed reference point (January 1, 1971). The answer is then the absolute difference of the two counts.

To convert a date to its day count:
1. Add the days in every full year from 1971 up to the year before the given year. A year has 366 days if it is a leap year, otherwise 365.
2. Add the days in every full month of the given year before the given month. February gets 29 days in a leap year.
3. Add the day of the month.

A year is a leap year if it is divisible by 4 and not by 100, or if it is divisible by 400. The year 2100 is not a leap year, and this rule handles it correctly.

```java
class Solution {
    public int daysBetweenDates(String date1, String date2) {
        return Math.abs(count(date1) - count(date2));
    }

    private boolean leap(int y) {
        return (y % 4 == 0 && y % 100 != 0) || y % 400 == 0;
    }

    private int count(String d) {
        int y = Integer.parseInt(d.substring(0, 4));
        int m = Integer.parseInt(d.substring(5, 7));
        int day = Integer.parseInt(d.substring(8, 10));
        int[] md = {31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31};
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 1971; i < y; i++) {
            total += leap(i) ? 366 : 365;
        }
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            total += md[i - 1];
            if (i == 2 && leap(y)) total++;
        }
        total += day;
        return total;
    }
}
```

