Approach:

1. The first element is the root. Split the rest into `left` (values smaller than the root) and `right` (values larger), keeping their original relative order.
2. Any valid reordering must keep the root first. After that, the left and right sequences can be interleaved in any way, as long as each keeps its own valid internal order.
3. So `ways(nums) = C(L + R, L) * ways(left) * ways(right)`, where `L` and `R` are the sizes of `left` and `right`. An empty or single-element list has exactly 1 way.
4. The original ordering is one of the counted ways, so the answer is `ways(nums) - 1`.
5. Precompute binomial coefficients with Pascal's triangle up to 1000, modulo `10^9 + 7`.
6. Recursion depth can reach 1000 in the worst case (a sorted array). That is fine for Java's default stack. Total work is O(n²).

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;
    long[][] C;

    public int numOfWays(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        C = new long[n + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            C[i][0] = 1;
            for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
                C[i][j] = (C[i - 1][j - 1] + C[i - 1][j]) % MOD;
            }
        }
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int x : nums) list.add(x);
        return (int) ((ways(list) - 1 + MOD) % MOD);
    }

    long ways(List<Integer> list) {
        if (list.size() <= 2) return 1;
        int root = list.get(0);
        List<Integer> left = new ArrayList<>(), right = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 1; i < list.size(); i++) {
            int v = list.get(i);
            if (v < root) left.add(v); else right.add(v);
        }
        long res = C[left.size() + right.size()][left.size()];
        res = res * ways(left) % MOD;
        res = res * ways(right) % MOD;
        return res;
    }
}
```

