inference_trace:
Approach: bitmask DP over the subsets of used elements.

1. Compute the total sum. If it is not divisible by `k`, return false. Otherwise the target sum per subset is `t = sum / k`.
2. Sort `nums`. If the largest element exceeds `t`, return false.
3. Let `dp[mask]` be the running sum of the current, partially filled bucket after using exactly the elements in `mask`. It is taken modulo `t`, and is `-1` if the state is unreachable.
4. Start from `dp[0] = 0`. For each reachable mask, try every unused element `i`:
   - If `dp[mask] + nums[i] <= t`, the state `mask | (1 << i)` is reachable with value `(dp[mask] + nums[i]) % t`.
   - Because `nums` is sorted, once `dp[mask] + nums[i] > t`, all later elements also overflow, so we can break.
5. The answer is whether the full mask is reachable.

This runs in O(2^n · n) time, which is fine for n ≤ 16.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public boolean canPartitionKSubsets(int[] nums, int k) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int x : nums) sum += x;
        if (sum % k != 0) return false;
        int t = sum / k;
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        int n = nums.length;
        if (nums[n - 1] > t) return false;
        int[] dp = new int[1 << n];
        Arrays.fill(dp, -1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] == -1) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (dp[mask] + nums[i] > t) break;
                if (dp[nm] == -1) {
                    dp[nm] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % t;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[(1 << n) - 1] == 0;
    }
}
```

