This reduces to a known result: choose `n = len/3` slices on a circular array so that no two chosen slices are adjacent, and maximize their sum. Every such set of non-adjacent slices can actually be achieved by some order of picks.

To handle the circle, solve the linear version twice and take the better answer:
- once on `slices[0 .. m-2]` (first slice allowed, last excluded),
- once on `slices[1 .. m-1]` (first slice excluded, last allowed).

For the linear version, use DP: `dp[i][j]` is the maximum sum using the first `i` elements of the range while picking exactly `j` of them, with no two adjacent.

Transition:
- `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i-2][j-1] + a[i-1])`
- Unreachable states are set to negative infinity.

Edge case: when `m = 3`, `n = 1`. Each range has length 2, and the DP correctly returns the best single element.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxSizeSlices(int[] slices) {
        int m = slices.length;
        int n = m / 3;
        return Math.max(solve(slices, 0, m - 2, n), solve(slices, 1, m - 1, n));
    }

    private int solve(int[] s, int lo, int hi, int n) {
        int len = hi - lo + 1;
        int NEG = Integer.MIN_VALUE / 2;
        int[][] dp = new int[len + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= len; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) dp[i][j] = NEG;
        }
        for (int i = 1; i <= len; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                int best = dp[i - 1][j];
                int prev = (i >= 2) ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 == 0 ? 0 : NEG);
                if (prev > NEG) best = Math.max(best, prev + s[lo + i - 1]);
                dp[i][j] = best;
            }
        }
        return dp[len][n];
    }
}
```

