inference_trace:
Use union-find over the people. For each request, find the roots `pu` and `pv` of the two people.

- If `pu == pv`, they are already connected, so the request succeeds.
- Otherwise, check every restriction `(x, y)`. Let `px` and `py` be their roots. Merging `pu` and `pv` would connect `x` and `y` exactly when `{px, py}` equals `{pu, pv}`. If any restriction matches, the request fails.
- If no restriction matches, union `pu` and `pv` and mark the request as successful.

Each request scans all restrictions once, so the total cost is O(requests × restrictions × α(n)), about 1e6 operations here.

```java
class Solution {
    int[] p;
    int find(int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) { p[x] = p[p[x]]; x = p[x]; }
        return x;
    }
    public boolean[] friendRequests(int n, int[][] restrictions, int[][] requests) {
        p = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i] = i;
        boolean[] res = new boolean[requests.length];
        for (int j = 0; j < requests.length; j++) {
            int pu = find(requests[j][0]), pv = find(requests[j][1]);
            if (pu == pv) { res[j] = true; continue; }
            boolean ok = true;
            for (int[] r : restrictions) {
                int px = find(r[0]), py = find(r[1]);
                if ((px == pu && py == pv) || (px == pv && py == pu)) { ok = false; break; }
            }
            if (ok) { p[pu] = pv; res[j] = true; }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

