inference_trace:
Use bottom-up dynamic programming. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when the suffix `s[i..]` matches the pattern suffix `p[j..]`.

**Base case:** `dp[m][n] = true`, since an empty string matches an empty pattern.

**Transition:** for `i` from `m` down to `0` and `j` from `n-1` down to `0`:
- Let `first = i < m && (p[j] == s[i] || p[j] == '.')`, meaning the current pattern character matches the current string character.
- If `p[j+1] == '*'`, there are two options:
  - Use the starred element zero times: `dp[i][j+2]`.
  - Use it at least once: `first && dp[i+1][j]`.
- Otherwise, the characters must match and both advance: `dp[i][j] = first && dp[i+1][j+1]`.

The answer is `dp[0][0]`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        dp[m][n] = true;
        for (int i = m; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                boolean first = i < m && (p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i) || p.charAt(j) == '.');
                if (j + 1 < n && p.charAt(j + 1) == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j + 2] || (first && dp[i + 1][j]);
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = first && dp[i + 1][j + 1];
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[0][0];
    }
}
```

