inference_trace:
Build an adjacency map from each value to its neighbours, then walk the chain from one end.

1. Every value appears in at most two pairs. Values range from -10^5 to 10^5, so store neighbours in arrays indexed by `value + 100000`, with a count per value.
2. An endpoint of the original array is a value that has exactly one neighbour. Pick any such value as the start.
3. Walk along the chain. At each step, move to whichever neighbour of the current value is not the previous value.
4. Values are unique and adjacent elements differ, so comparing against the previous value is enough to avoid going backwards. It also works when values are 0 or negative, because we compare actual values rather than using a sentinel.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] restoreArray(int[][] adjacentPairs) {
        int OFF = 100000;
        int size = 200001;
        int[] a = new int[size];
        int[] b = new int[size];
        int[] cnt = new int[size];
        for (int[] p : adjacentPairs) {
            int u = p[0] + OFF, v = p[1] + OFF;
            if (cnt[u] == 0) a[u] = p[1]; else b[u] = p[1];
            cnt[u]++;
            if (cnt[v] == 0) a[v] = p[0]; else b[v] = p[0];
            cnt[v]++;
        }
        int n = adjacentPairs.length + 1;
        int start = 0;
        for (int[] p : adjacentPairs) {
            if (cnt[p[0] + OFF] == 1) { start = p[0]; break; }
            if (cnt[p[1] + OFF] == 1) { start = p[1]; break; }
        }
        int[] res = new int[n];
        res[0] = start;
        res[1] = a[start + OFF];
        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            int cur = res[i - 1] + OFF;
            int prev = res[i - 2];
            res[i] = (a[cur] == prev) ? b[cur] : a[cur];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

