inference_trace:
I'll use a bottom-up DP over substring lengths.

**State.** `dp[len][i][j]` is true when `s1.substring(i, i+len)` can be scrambled into `s2.substring(j, j+len)`.

**Base case.** For `len = 1`, `dp[1][i][j]` is true exactly when `s1[i] == s2[j]`.

**Transition.** For `len > 1`, try every split point `k` from 1 to `len-1`. The pair is scramblable if either option holds:
- **No swap:** `dp[k][i][j]` and `dp[len-k][i+k][j+k]`.
- **Swap:** `dp[k][i][j+len-k]` and `dp[len-k][i+k][j]`.

**Complexity.** With n ≤ 30 this is O(n⁴), about 810,000 operations, which is easily fast enough.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isScramble(String s1, String s2) {
        int n = s1.length();
        if (n != s2.length()) return false;
        boolean[][][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][n][n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
                dp[1][i][j] = s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j);
        for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j + len <= n; j++) {
                    boolean ok = false;
                    for (int k = 1; k < len && !ok; k++) {
                        if (dp[k][i][j] && dp[len - k][i + k][j + k]) ok = true;
                        else if (dp[k][i][j + len - k] && dp[len - k][i + k][j]) ok = true;
                    }
                    dp[len][i][j] = ok;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][0][0];
    }
}
```

