Sort the products. Then, for each prefix of `searchWord`, binary search for the first product that is lexicographically greater than or equal to the prefix. Starting from that position, take at most three consecutive products that start with the prefix.

Since products are sorted, every product sharing the prefix sits in one contiguous block. The binary search lands at the start of that block, so the next (up to three) matching products are the lexicographically smallest ones.

I'll use explicit imports for `java.util`.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> suggestedProducts(String[] products, String searchWord) {
        Arrays.sort(products);
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int n = products.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < searchWord.length(); i++) {
            sb.append(searchWord.charAt(i));
            String prefix = sb.toString();
            int lo = 0, hi = n;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
                if (products[mid].compareTo(prefix) < 0) lo = mid + 1;
                else hi = mid;
            }
            List<String> cur = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int j = lo; j < n && j < lo + 3; j++) {
                if (products[j].startsWith(prefix)) cur.add(products[j]);
                else break;
            }
            res.add(cur);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

