Treat the grid as one flat array of length `total = m * n`. Shifting the grid `k` times is the same as rotating that flat array right by `k` positions.

1. Reduce the shift with `k % total`, since shifting by `total` returns the original grid.
2. For each output position `idx`, the value comes from the source index `(idx - k + total) % total`.
3. Convert that flat source index back to a cell: row `src / n`, column `src % n`.
4. Fill the result row by row.

This runs in O(m·n) time. Imports are included because LeetCode's environment normally provides `java.util`, but the explicit import keeps the code self-contained.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> shiftGrid(int[][] grid, int k) {
        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
        int total = m * n;
        k %= total;
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            List<Integer> row = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                int idx = i * n + j;
                int src = ((idx - k) % total + total) % total;
                row.add(grid[src / n][src % n]);
            }
            res.add(row);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

