Allocate a result array of length `2n`. For each index `i` from `0` to `n - 1`:
- put `nums[i]` (the x-value) at position `2i`;
- put `nums[i + n]` (the matching y-value) at position `2i + 1`.

This interleaves the two halves in order. It runs in O(n) time and uses O(n) extra space.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] shuffle(int[] nums, int n) {
        int[] res = new int[2 * n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            res[2 * i] = nums[i];
            res[2 * i + 1] = nums[i + n];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
