Each value is at most 10^4, so its bit count is at most 14. That lets us pack both sort keys into one integer and sort with a plain array sort.

1. For each element, build the key `bitCount(x) * 100000 + x`. Since `x < 100000`, the bit count dominates the ordering, and the value itself breaks ties in ascending order.
2. Sort the keys.
3. Recover each original value with `key % 100000`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] sortByBits(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] keys = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            keys[i] = Integer.bitCount(arr[i]) * 100000 + arr[i];
        }
        java.util.Arrays.sort(keys);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            keys[i] %= 100000;
        }
        return keys;
    }
}
```

