Greedy approach: build the string one character at a time.

- At each step, write the letter that has more remaining copies.
- Exception: if the last two characters are already that letter, write the other letter instead.
- Since the problem guarantees a valid answer exists, this greedy always succeeds.

```java
class Solution {
    public String strWithout3a3b(int a, int b) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (a > 0 || b > 0) {
            int n = sb.length();
            boolean writeA;
            if (n >= 2 && sb.charAt(n - 1) == sb.charAt(n - 2)) {
                writeA = sb.charAt(n - 1) == 'b';
            } else {
                writeA = a >= b;
            }
            if (writeA && a == 0) writeA = false;
            if (!writeA && b == 0) writeA = true;
            if (writeA) {
                sb.append('a');
                a--;
            } else {
                sb.append('b');
                b--;
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
