inference_trace:
For each row, count the soldiers with a binary search for the first `0`. This works because every row is all `1`s followed by all `0`s.

Then encode each row as a single key: `count * 1000 + index`. Since `m <= 100`, the index part never spills into the count part. Sorting these keys orders rows by soldier count first, then by index on ties.

After sorting, the first `k` keys give the answer. Recover each row index with `key % 1000`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] kWeakestRows(int[][] mat, int k) {
        int m = mat.length;
        int[] keys = new int[m];
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            int lo = 0, hi = mat[i].length;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
                if (mat[i][mid] == 1) lo = mid + 1;
                else hi = mid;
            }
            keys[i] = lo * 1000 + i;
        }
        java.util.Arrays.sort(keys);
        int[] res = new int[k];
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) res[i] = keys[i] % 1000;
        return res;
    }
}
```

