Use the greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the last `*`:

1. Walk through `s` with pointer `i` and through `p` with pointer `j`.
2. If `p[j]` equals `s[i]` or is `?`, advance both pointers.
3. If `p[j]` is `*`, record its position as `star` and the current `i` as `match`. Then advance only `j`, so the star first matches the empty sequence.
4. On a mismatch, if a `*` was seen earlier, backtrack: let that star absorb one more character. Set `j = star + 1`, increment `match`, and set `i = match`.
5. If there is a mismatch and no earlier `*`, return false.
6. Once `s` is consumed, skip any trailing `*` in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` reaches the end of `p`.

This runs in O(n·m) time in the worst case and uses O(1) extra space.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, match = 0;
        int n = s.length(), m = p.length();
        while (i < n) {
            if (j < m && (p.charAt(j) == '?' || p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i))) {
                i++; j++;
            } else if (j < m && p.charAt(j) == '*') {
                star = j;
                match = i;
                j++;
            } else if (star != -1) {
                j = star + 1;
                match++;
                i = match;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while (j < m && p.charAt(j) == '*') j++;
        return j == m;
    }
}
```

