Use a breadth-first search, since every edge (one-letter change) has equal cost and BFS finds the shortest sequence.

1. Put all words from `wordList` into a set `dict`. If `endWord` is not in it, return 0.
2. Start the BFS from `beginWord` with level 1, since the count includes the starting word.
3. For each word taken from the queue, try replacing each position with every letter `a`–`z`.
   - If the new word equals `endWord`, return the current level + 1.
   - If the new word is in `dict`, remove it from `dict` (this marks it visited) and add it to the queue.
4. Each word is processed once, and generating neighbors costs about `L * 26` per word, so the total time is `O(N * L * 26)`.
5. If the queue empties without reaching `endWord`, return 0.

`java.util.*` is imported so that `List`, `Set`, `HashSet`, `Queue` and `ArrayDeque` are available.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int ladderLength(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(wordList);
        if (!dict.contains(endWord)) return 0;
        Queue<String> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        q.add(beginWord);
        dict.remove(beginWord);
        int level = 1;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            for (int s = 0; s < size; s++) {
                char[] arr = q.poll().toCharArray();
                for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
                    char orig = arr[i];
                    for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                        if (c == orig) continue;
                        arr[i] = c;
                        String nw = new String(arr);
                        if (dict.contains(nw)) {
                            if (nw.equals(endWord)) return level + 1;
                            dict.remove(nw);
                            q.add(nw);
                        }
                    }
                    arr[i] = orig;
                }
            }
            level++;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
